After karr #35 moved the plaintext emitter into the format handlers, lib/File/SOPS.pm has no code left that touches either module:

    use JSON::MaybeXS;   # line 14
    use YAML::XS ();     # line 15

The only remaining mentions are POD examples (JSON->true) and comments. Both modules are still loaded transitively -- Format::JSON and Encrypted.pm use JSON::MaybeXS, Format::YAML uses YAML::XS -- so removing the two lines is a no-op at runtime, but leaving them says File::SOPS.pm emits YAML and JSON itself, which is exactly the impression #35 removed.

Left alone deliberately in #35 rather than folded into a change that had to be provably byte-neutral. cpanfile keeps both as direct prerequisites either way (they are, via the handlers).
Half stale as of karr #38: File::SOPS.pm now DOES use YAML::XS - _load_creation_rules calls YAML::XS::Load to read a .sops.yaml (a config file, not a SOPS document, so it does not go through the format handlers). JSON::MaybeXS is still loaded with nothing using it. Re-check both before acting.
Premise checked and it does not hold. Nothing removed; both use lines stay, each now carrying a comment saying why.

`use YAML::XS ()` (line 15): live since karr #38 -- _load_creation_rules calls YAML::XS::Load on a .sops.yaml, which is a config file and so does not go through Format::YAML. Already noted on this ticket; confirmed.

`use JSON::MaybeXS` (line 14): no code in File/SOPS.pm calls encode_json, decode_json or JSON(), and nothing is re-exported -- `use namespace::clean` strips all three (`perl -Ilib -MFile::SOPS -e "print defined &File::SOPS::JSON ? 1 : 0"` prints 0). Nor is it what guarantees JSON::PP::Boolean for decrypt: that comes from File::SOPS::Encrypted, whose own `use JSON::MaybeXS` sits in the same file as the JSON->true call that needs it, and which works with File/SOPS.pm never loaded at all.

What the line actually does is decide the JSON backend. JSON::MaybeXS binds once, to whichever of Cpanel::JSON::XS / JSON::XS is already in %INC; File::SOPS::Encrypted loads CryptX, which loads JSON.pm and with it JSON::XS. So the line being ABOVE `use File::SOPS::Encrypted` is what lets JSON::MaybeXS state its own preference:

    perl -Ilib -MFile::SOPS -e "print JSON::MaybeXS::JSON()"   # Cpanel::JSON::XS
    ... with line 14 deleted                                   # JSON::XS

and the two do not emit the same bytes. Measured end to end through encrypt(format => json, unencrypted_suffix => _unencrypted):

    with line 14      "ratio_unencrypted" : 1.0
    without line 14   "ratio_unencrypted" : 1

Same for -0.0 vs -0, and the same difference reaches Format::JSON->emit, i.e. what decrypt_file and edit write. Deleting the line is a wire change, not a cleanup -- API lane hands it on rather than doing it.

sops 3.13.3 accepts both (sops -d exits 0, MAC verifies, because Encrypted::value_to_bytes already hashes NV 1.0 as "1"), but writes `1` and `-0` itself -- JSON::XS rendering, not the one we land on. That plus the fact that the ordering is not even a guarantee (a caller who loads JSON::XS before File::SOPS gets JSON::XS) is now karr #56, for the wire lane.

Left behind: t/11-api-edges.t section (f), two checks in a fresh perl that loads only File::SOPS -- one asserts File::SOPS picks the same backend JSON::MaybeXS would on its own (goes red exactly on this deletion, verified against a tree with line 14 removed), one asserts decrypt still returns overloaded JSON::PP::Boolean there (stays green with the line removed, which is the point: the boolean guarantee is Encrypted.pms, not this lines).

Also found: karr #57, the POD boolean examples use JSON->true and never say the caller needs JSON::MaybeXS; they only work today because CryptX drags JSON.pm in.

